217	<body>
218	<div class="wrap">
219	
220	  <header>
221	    <div class="grow">
222	      <h1>Germany's cross-border power balance</h1>
223	      <p class="sub">The price of German power swings several-fold within a day, and
224	      cross-border flows follow it: in when it is dear, out when it is cheap. The cheap
225	      hours are cheap <em>because</em> wind and solar are running.</p>
226	    </div>
227	    <button class="ghost" id="theme" title="Switch theme">Theme</button>
228	  </header>
229	
230	  <div id="err"></div>
231	  <div id="notice" hidden></div>
232	
233	  <section class="hero" id="hero" hidden>
234	    <div>
235	      <div class="lede-label" id="heroLabel">The gap between buying and selling</div>
236	      <div class="lede-value" id="heroValue">—</div>
237	      <div class="lede-note" id="heroNote"></div>
238	    </div>
239	    <div class="tiles" id="tiles"></div>
240	  </section>
241	
242	  <section class="card" id="cardRead" hidden>
243	    <p class="read">Three facts that stop the numbers above being read as a verdict.
244	    <b>Every trade cleared at a price its seller had bid</b>, so nothing here was sold
245	    below cost and nothing was bought unwillingly — a uniform-price auction cannot do
246	    either. <b>The cheap hours are cheap because wind and solar are running</b>; without
247	    them those hours would price like the expensive ones, and German consumers would pay
248	    more, not less. <b>And importing in the dear hours lowers the German price</b> rather
249	    than raising it, because the imported megawatt-hour displaces a more expensive German
250	    plant. What is left is a real fact with real consequences: the value of a megawatt-hour
251	    in Germany varies several-fold within the day.</p>
252	  </section>
253	
254	  <div class="filters" id="filters" hidden>
255	    <div class="seg" id="ranges" role="group" aria-label="Time range"></div>
256	    <div class="spacer"></div>
257	    <div class="range-note" id="rangeNote"></div>
258	  </div>
259	
260	  <section class="card" id="cardBalance" hidden>
261	    <div class="card-head">
262	      <div class="grow">
263	        <h2>Daily balance</h2>
264	        <p class="hint">The value of what left minus the value of what arrived, both priced
265	        at the German hourly price. A valuation, not a cash flow.</p>
266	      </div>
267	      <div class="key">
268	        <span><i style="background:var(--out)"></i>arrivals worth more</span>
269	        <span><i style="background:var(--in)"></i>departures worth more</span>
270	      </div>
271	    </div>
272	    <div class="plot" id="plotBalance"></div>
273	  </section>
274	
275	  <section class="card" id="cardPrice" hidden>
276	    <div class="card-head">
277	      <div class="grow">
278	        <h2>When power arrives, and when it leaves</h2>
279	        <p class="hint" id="priceHint">Volume-weighted German price in the hours Germany
280	        imported, against the hours it exported. Both are the same price curve — the gap
281	        is timing.</p>
282	      </div>
283	      <div class="key">
284	        <span><i class="line" style="background:var(--out)"></i><span id="keyImp">paid for power kept</span></span>
285	        <span><i class="line" style="background:var(--in)"></i><span id="keyExp">earned on own output</span></span>
286	      </div>
287	    </div>
288	    <div class="plot" id="plotPrice"></div>
289	  </section>
290	
291	  <section class="card" id="cardResidualDaily" hidden>
292	    <div class="card-head">
293	      <div class="grow">
294	        <h2>What wind and solar left to cover</h2>
295	        <p class="hint" id="resDailyHint">The same days as the chart above: how much
296	        demand wind and solar left for everything else to cover. Tall bars line up with
297	        the expensive days.</p>
298	      </div>
299	      <div class="key">
300	        <span><i style="background:var(--out)"></i>net importer</span>
301	        <span><i style="background:var(--in)"></i>net exporter</span>
302	      </div>
303	    </div>
304	    <div class="plot" id="plotResidualDaily"></div>
305	  </section>
306	
307	  <section class="card" id="cardWind" hidden>
308	    <div class="card-head">
309	      <div class="grow">
310	        <h2>Wind goes missing below zero</h2>
311	        <p class="hint" id="windHint"></p>
312	      </div>
313	      <div class="key">
314	        <span><i style="background:var(--out)"></i>the negative hour</span>
315	        <span><i style="background:var(--thru);opacity:.55"></i>control hours</span>
316	      </div>
317	    </div>
318	    <div class="plot" id="plotWind"></div>
319	    <p class="read">The shape is the argument. Weather is smooth in time, so a
320	    badly-forecast windy spell cannot produce a shortfall one hour wide — it would slope
321	    in and out across the neighbouring hours. A shutdown triggered by the hour's own
322	    price can, and this one does: the deficit collapses by an order of magnitude the hour
323	    either side, and the control hours scatter around zero in both directions.
324	    <span id="windControl"></span></p>
325	    <p class="read">What this cannot tell you is <em>who</em> stopped. Below zero a
326	    plant selling into the market loses money on every megawatt-hour, so feathering the
327	    blades is the owner's own call — but only for plants whose support premium is
328	    actually withdrawn, which under § 51 EEG depends on commissioning date: never for
329	    plants older than 2016, after six, four or one consecutive negative hours for later
330	    vintages, and from the first negative quarter-hour only for plants commissioned since
331	    February 2025. Curtailment <em>ordered</em> by the grid operator, because northern
332	    wind cannot reach southern demand, lands in the same high-wind hours and looks
333	    identical here. Price-conditioning cannot separate the two; the ordered part is
334	    counted in the redispatch spending under
335	    <a href="#cardCosts">both sides of the ledger</a>.</p>
336	    <p class="read" id="windSeason"></p>
337	  </section>
338	
339	  <section class="card" id="cardVolume" hidden>
340	    <div class="card-head">
341	      <div class="grow">
342	        <h2>Volumes</h2>
343	        <p class="hint" id="volHint">Energy crossing the border each day.</p>
344	      </div>
345	      <div class="key">
346	        <span><i style="background:var(--out)"></i>imported and used here</span>
347	        <span><i style="background:var(--thru)"></i>in transit</span>
348	        <span><i style="background:var(--in)"></i>exported from German plants</span>
349	      </div>
350	    </div>
351	    <div class="plot" id="plotVolume"></div>
352	  </section>
353	
354	  <section class="card" id="cardDay" hidden>
355	    <div class="card-head">
356	      <div class="grow">
357	        <h2 id="dayTitle">How one day's price happens</h2>
358	        <p class="hint" id="dayHint"></p>
359	      </div>
360	      <div class="key" id="dayKey"></div>
361	    </div>
362	    <div class="plot" id="plotDay"></div>
363	    <p class="read" id="dayNote"></p>
364	  </section>
365	
366	  <section class="card" id="cardBorder" hidden>
367	    <div class="card-head">
368	      <div class="grow">
369	        <h2>By border</h2>
370	        <p class="hint">Balance per neighbouring bidding zone, summed over the window.</p>
371	      </div>
372	    </div>
373	    <div class="plot" id="plotBorder"></div>
374	  </section>
375	
376	  <section class="card" id="cardCosts" hidden>
377	    <div class="card-head">
378	      <div class="grow">
379	        <h2>Congestion, both sides of the ledger</h2>
380	        <p class="hint">What the borders earned in congestion rent, against what the
381	        transmission operators spent keeping the grid within limits. Related economics,
382	        but not one ledger: the rent is day-ahead income on the eleven DE-LU borders,
383	        the spending is Germany-wide and includes the internal grid. This one ignores the
384	        range above and shows every month where both sides exist: the spending series
385	        lags roughly three months behind, so the most recent months on the page have no
386	        partner yet.</p>
387	      </div>
388	      <div class="key">
389	        <span><i style="background:var(--in)"></i>rent earned on borders</span>
390	        <span><i style="background:var(--out)"></i>spent on grid security</span>
391	      </div>
392	    </div>
393	    <div class="plot" id="plotCosts"></div>
394	    <details>
395	      <summary>Where does this money come from, and where does it go?</summary>
396	      <div class="flows">
397	        <div>
398	          <h3><i style="background:var(--in)"></i>The rent, in and out</h3>
399	          <p><b>In:</b> when a border is full, the two sides clear at different prices.
400	          Buyers in the dearer zone pay their own zonal price and sellers in the cheaper
401	          zone receive theirs; the gap on each megawatt-hour that crossed is collected by
402	          the coupling mechanism and paid to the two transmission operators on that
403	          border, normally split evenly. Nobody is billed for it separately — it is
404	          already inside the price the dearer zone paid.</p>
405	          <p><b>Out:</b> the operators cannot keep it. Article 19 of Regulation (EU)
406	          2019/943 puts it to two priority uses first — guaranteeing that the capacity
407	          they sold stays firm, including compensation when it does not, and maintaining
408	          or increasing cross-zonal capacity, whether by remedial actions or by building
409	          interconnection. Only once those are satisfied may it be counted as income when
410	          network tariffs are set, and anything left has to sit in a separate account
411	          until it can be spent on those purposes.</p>
412	        </div>
413	        <div>
414	          <h3><i style="background:var(--out)"></i>The spending, in and out</h3>
415	          <p><b>Out:</b> the grid inside Germany constrains flows the market ignored, so
416	          the operator intervenes. In redispatch it orders one power station down and
417	          another up on the far side of the bottleneck, and compensates both for the
418	          change. The same budget covers stations held in reserve outside the market for
419	          exactly this, loads paid to be interruptible, and countertrading — buying and
420	          selling across a border to relieve a constraint.</p>
421	          <p><b>In:</b> from every electricity customer. The operators recover it through
422	          network charges, passed to the distribution operators and on to consumers,
423	          spread across all German users by a mechanism descended from the § 19 StromNEV
424	          levy. Domestic and industrial alike, in proportion to network use.</p>
425	        </div>
426	      </div>
427	      <p class="read">So both ends of this chart reach the same place: the grid fee on a
428	      bill. Rent can only lower it after the capacity obligations are met; redispatch
429	      raises it. And that fee is part of the fixed two-thirds of a household bill in the
430	      panel below — the part that does not move when the wholesale price does.</p>
431	    </details>
432	  </section>
433	
434	  <section class="card" id="cardLevels" hidden>
435	    <div class="card-head">
436	      <div class="grow">
